@meta
id: 26-15
title: A terminal in a window
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: terminal
par: 45
minutes: 7
vscode: the integrated terminal, which is the only way you have ever done this
open: ops-notes.md

@brief
The ops note wants the number of lines in `orders.csv` written after the
colon on the last line.

Do it the long way first, deliberately, because the long way is the thing
being taught: open a terminal inside vim with `:term`, run the count in
it, read the number, close the terminal, and write the number down.

A terminal buffer is a real shell running in a vim window. It is an
ordinary window — `Ctrl-W` moves between it and your file, `:q` will not
close it while the job runs, and `exit` ends the shell and the buffer with
it. While the job has the keyboard, every key you press goes to the shell,
including `Ctrl-W` and `Esc`, so there is one key that gets you out:
`Ctrl-W N` puts the terminal into normal mode, where the scrollback is
just text you can search, yank and read with `/`, `G` and `y`.

Do not change `orders.csv`.

@keys
| `:term` | open a shell in a new window |
| `Ctrl-W N` | terminal into normal mode — scroll, search, yank its output |
| `i` | back to talking to the shell, from terminal normal mode |
| `exit` | end the shell and close the buffer |
| `Ctrl-W w` | move to the other window |

@hints
`:term` splits the window and drops you in a shell, in the same directory
as the file. Run the count there.
--
`wc -l orders.csv` in the terminal prints the number. Then `exit` closes
the shell and puts you back in the note.
--
`:term` Enter, `wc -l orders.csv` Enter, `exit` Enter, then `G`,
`A` space `12`, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    :term<Enter>  wc -l orders.csv<Enter>  exit<Enter>  G  A 12<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Around forty keystrokes, a window that opened and closed, and a number
you carried across in your head.

Now the short way:

    :$s/$/ 12/     — if you already knew the number
    :r !wc -l < orders.csv   — read the answer straight into the file
    :w !wc -l      — ask about the buffer you are in, without a terminal

That comparison is the lesson. `:term` exists, it is genuinely useful,
and it is almost never the right tool for a question about text. Anything
of the form "what does this command say about my file" is `:r !` or
`:w !` or a filter, and those are one command with no window management
and no retyping of an answer you read off a screen.

What `:term` is actually for is the things that are not questions:
watching a test suite run while you edit, an interactive `git rebase -i`,
a REPL you keep alive across an afternoon, an `ssh` session. Long-running
and interactive — anything where you need the program to still be there
in a minute.

Two facts worth carrying. `Ctrl-W N` is the way out: in terminal-job mode
the shell owns every key, and `N` is what hands the window back to vim so
you can scroll the output, search it, and yank a stack trace straight
into a commit message. And `:term cmd` skips the shell entirely —
`:term npm test` runs the suite in a window and leaves the output there
when it finishes.

Neovim's version is `:terminal` with `Ctrl-\ Ctrl-N` to leave job mode
rather than `Ctrl-W N`. Chapter 31 covers the differences that matter.

@start ops-notes.md
# Ops notes

Everything here is written down by hand after the fact, which is why it
disagrees with reality about half the time.

## Line counts

orders.csv:

